      "\n",
      "Title: The Most Popular Dog Breeds in the U.S. in 2024 | Rover.com\n",
      "URL: https://www.rover.com/blog/popular-dog-breeds/\n",
      "ID: https://www.rover.com/blog/popular-dog-breeds/\n",
      "Score: None\n",
      "Published Date: 2024-05-10T18:00:20.000Z\n",
      "Author: \n",
      "Image: https://www.rover.com/blog/wp-content/uploads/Her-Image_Social_Dog.png\n",
      "Favicon: https://www.rover.com/blog/wp-content/themes/the-dog-people/assets/icons/favicons/favicon-32x32.png\n",
      "Extras: None\n",
      "Subpages: None\n",
      "Text: - [Skip to primary navigation](https://www.rover.com/blog/popular-dog-breeds/#primary-navigation)\n",
      "- [Skip to main content](https://www.rover.com/blog/popular-dog-breeds/#primary-content)\n",
      "\n",
      "Search Rover Blog\n",
      "\n",
      "![](https://www.rover.com/blog/wp-content/uploads/Hero_Copy_US-CA-1.png)\n",
      "\n",
      "Humans have bred dogs for their skills, companionship, and looks for thousands of years, resulting in hundreds of recognized breeds around the world—and even more unofficial dog breeds and mixes. With this many breeds to choose from, there’s a pup for just about everyone.\n",
      "\n",
      "And while every dog is unique, all those years of breeding can impart certain truths: [Herding dogs](https://www.rover.com/blog/train-herding-dog/) love to herd, pointers love to point, and lap dogs, well, they often love to curl up on a nice lap. In turn, 62% of pet parents say breed is an important factor when [adding a new dog to their families](https://www.rover.com/blog/first-time-dog-owner/).\n",
      "\n",
      "The majority (88%) of pet parents also agree that knowing more about their dog’s precise mix of breeds would help them understand their needs better, and over half (56%) believe they’d feel more connected to their pet if they learned about their ancestry.\n",
      "\n",
      "Every person may have their breed preferences, but only a few breeds are top dog when it comes to overall popularity. We dug into the millions of pets in [Rover’s](https://www.rover.com/) database and uncovered the most popular dog breeds of 2024—plus, trending and less common dog breeds that are on the rise too.\n",
      "\n",
      "## Dog Breed Popularity Across the Nation\n",
      "\n",
      "![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20296%20152'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "#1\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Mixed\n",
      "\n",
      "Often hardy and always unique, mixed breed dogs continually top lists of the most popular dog types in America. There’s a mixed pup out there for just about everyone!\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/?post_type=breed&p=124614)\n",
      "\n",
      "#2\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Labrador Retriever\n",
      "\n",
      "Labs are one of the most popular breeds in the US. These high-spirited sporting dogs are perfect for people who love swimming, jogging, or hunting.\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/labrador-retriever/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#3\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Goldendoodle\n",
      "\n",
      "The Goldendoodle is a fairly new crossbreed that’s growing in popularity. They’re known for their teddy bear looks, low-shedding coats, and friendly personalities.\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/goldendoodle/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#4\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Golden Retriever\n",
      "\n",
      "Intelligence and a friendly disposition make goldens one of America’s most popular dog breeds. These smart pups do well with obedience training and make great hunters.\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/golden-retriever/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#5\n",
      "\n",
      "#### German Shepherd\n",
      "\n",
      "Intelligent and protective, German Shepherds make loyal companions. These dogs bond well with their owners through physical activities like herding, tracking, and agility.\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/german-shepherd/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#6\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Chihuahua\n",
      "\n",
      "Want a lot of personality in a compact package? Chihuahuas are ideal dogs for city dwellers, but they need training to temper their “big dog” attitudes.\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/chihuahua/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#7\n",
      "\n",
      "#### French Bulldog\n",
      "\n",
      "Stocky like an English Bulldog but not as large, Frenchies are great for homes of all sizes. They don’t need a ton of exercise—nice walks and trips to the dog park work well.\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/french-bulldog/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#8\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Dachshund\n",
      "\n",
      "These spunky pups are full of personality. Ever curious and alert, Dachshunds are notoriously stubborn and require patience to train, though the results are totally worth it.\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/dachshund/)\n",
      "\n",
      "![A photo of two French Bulldogs on Rover](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20500%20452'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "### Ellie and Gus, the French Bulldogs\n",
      "\n",
      "“Ellie and Gus are inseparable and have caused me to fall in love with French Bulldogs as a breed! Though they are from different breeders and parts of the country, they have developed a bond and love for each other that is unbreakable. Gus seems to act like he is 10 but is only 2 with his stoic nature and observant approach to life, and on the other end of the spectrum, you have Ellie, a complete love bug always looking for a cuddle or to wrestle. Together they are a hysterical pair that always keeps me laughing, loving, and remembering to not take life so seriously!”\n",
      "\n",
      "— Austin, Dog Parent on Rover, [Austin TX](https://www.rover.com/austin--tx--dog-boarding/)\n",
      "\n",
      "## The Top Trending Dog Breeds in 2024\n",
      "\n",
      "![Green arrow](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20402%2070'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "#1\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Miniature Poodle\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/mini-poodle/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#2\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Bernedoodle\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/bernedoodle/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#3\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Cavalier King Charles Spaniel\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#4\n",
      "\n",
      "#### French Bulldog\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/french-bulldog/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#5\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Dachshund\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/dachshund/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#6\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Toy Poodle\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/toy-poodle/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#7\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Poodle\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/poodle/)\n",
      "\n",
      "#8\n",
      "\n",
      "#### American Bully\n",
      "\n",
      "[Learn more](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/american-bully/)\n",
      "\n",
      "![A photo of a miniature poodle on Rover](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20500%20453'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "### Coby the Miniature Poodle\n",
      "\n",
      "“Why I love this breed? Simple. Small dog, Big City, Beauty and Brains. Coby is a great dog to have living in an NYC apartment as he doesn’t require too much space to have loads of fun, and he meets the size requirement for most NYC apartments. While very energetic, Mini Poodles are also easygoing and will nap all day with you on a rainy day. Seldom does he step out and not get compliments on how handsome he is and his beautiful chocolate coat. He’s got the brains to back the beauty. Mini Poodles are highly intelligent and easy to train, and Coby has proven as much.”\n",
      "\n",
      "— Christina, Dog Parent on Rover, [New York City NY](https://www.rover.com/new-york-city--ny--dog-boarding/)\n",
      "\n",
      "## The Top Four Rarest Dog Breeds in the US\n",
      "\n",
      "#1\n",
      "\n",
      "#### King Shepherd\n",
      "\n",
      "#2\n",
      "\n",
      "#### English Toy Terrier (Black and Tan)\n",
      "\n",
      "#3\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Canaan Dog\n",
      "\n",
      "#4\n",
      "\n",
      "#### Black Russian Terrier\n",
      "\n",
      "![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20296%20152'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "## Personality Over Pedigree\n",
      "\n",
      "According to US pet parents, their [dog’s personality](https://www.rover.com/blog/dog-personalities-a-to-z/) and behavior is nearly equally important as their pedigree. In fact, 61% of pet parents picked their dog based on assumed personality or behavioral traits characteristic of their breed or breed mix.\n",
      "\n",
      "## For Man’s Best Friend, It’s All About Loyalty\n",
      "\n",
      "According to 69% of pet parents, loyalty is the most desirable personality trait a dog can have. (They don’t call ‘em man’s best friend for no reason!) Additionally, 68% of pet parents believe their dogs actually possess this trait.\n",
      "\n",
      "While any dog can exhibit loyalty, pet parents agree that some breeds are more likely to be your ride-or-die: in third place is the small but fierce [Chihuahua](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/chihuahua/). Next up, in the second spot, is the intelligent, hard working [German Shepherd](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/german-shepherd/). And the #1 most loyal dog breed is the devoted [American Pitbull Terrier](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/american-pit-bull-terrier/).\n",
      "\n",
      "Other characteristics pet parents value include a friendly, social nature (68% of pet parents agree) and playfulness (63%). You’ll find the Chihuahua frequently on our “Best Of” lists for [first time dog parent](https://www.rover.com/blog/best-dog-breeds-first-time-owners-in/) s and [low maintenance breeds](https://www.rover.com/blog/low-maintenance-dog-breeds/). But these top breeds are also featured in our [most loyal dog breeds](https://www.rover.com/blog/most-loyal-dogs/) list.\n",
      "\n",
      "![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%201024%20763'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20296%20152'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "## Anxious Pet Parents, Anxious Pups\n",
      "\n",
      "Like us, dogs are complex and can sometimes adopt challenging traits and behaviors. The #1 most common is separation anxiety, according to 42% of pet parents. [Separation anxiety](https://www.rover.com/blog/separation-anxiety-in-dogs/) is also the second most likely trait to be shared by dogs and their humans (47%).\n",
      "\n",
      "Of this year’s top 10 most popular breeds, the DNA of most [Dachshunds](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/dachshund/) and [Yorkshire Terriers](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/yorkshire-terrier/) suggests they are more likely to experience separation anxiety (according to Caitlyn Bruns, animal geneticist for [Know Your Pet DNA by Ancestry](https://petdna.ancestry.com/about?o_xid=139706&o_lid=139706&o_sch=Partners&utm_medium=partnerships&utm_source=rover&utm_campaign=Partnerships-US-Rover-HubPage-2024)).\n",
      "\n",
      "Other common behavioral challenges reported by pet parents include [fear of loud noises](https://www.rover.com/blog/sound-desensitization-protocol-for-dogs-with-noise-anxiety/) (36%), [stress at the vet](https://www.rover.com/blog/taking-an-anxious-dog-to-the-vet/) (25%), [guard dog behavior](https://www.rover.com/blog/6-tips-training-territorial-dog/) (24%), and [possessiveness](https://www.rover.com/blog/how-to-handle-highly-possessive-dog/) (23%).\n",
      "\n",
      "![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%201024%20875'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20296%20152'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "## The Traits We Share With Our Dogs\n",
      "\n",
      "The majority (71%) of pet parents agree that a person’s choice of dog breed is an extension of their own personality, and 83% believe that most dogs’ personalities are in fact influenced by their pet parents.\n",
      "\n",
      "It’s not surprising then that 52% of pet parents say they and their dog act alike or share behavioral traits and interests. Of them, the most commonly shared traits are reportedly: loyalty (71%), playfulness (65%), [intelligence](https://www.rover.com/blog/smartest-dog-breeds/) (61%), and friendliness (61%).\n",
      "\n",
      "Additionally, the majority (62%) of pet parents with socially [anxious dogs](https://www.rover.com/blog/dog-anxiety/) say they share this behavioral trait. The same goes for those with separation anxiety (47%).\n",
      "\n",
      "Other behavioral challenges most commonly shared between people and their dogs include stress at the vet or doctor (32%), [fear or aggression toward strangers](https://www.rover.com/blog/stranger-danger-barking-rescue-dog-advice/) (27%), guard dog behavior (25%), and fear of loud noises (25%).\n",
      "\n",
      "What’s the most human-like dog breed, you ask? When asked which dog breed has the most human-like qualities (looks, personality, behaviors, etc.), pet parents voted the [Golden Retriever](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/golden-retriever/) as number one. Coming in second is the [German Shepherd](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/german-shepherd/), followed by the [Labrador Retriever](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/labrador-retriever/) in third.\n",
      "\n",
      "![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20812%201024'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "## Top Trending Dog Breeds by Country\n",
      "\n",
      "![Green arrow](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20402%2070'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "![The top trending breed by country in 2024](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%201024%20536'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "While some breeds are perennially popular, others are just starting to make their mark as trending breeds. These dogs rose quickly in popularity in 2024, becoming the top trending breed in their countries.\n",
      "\n",
      "In the US, the [Miniature Poodle](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/mini-poodle/) is the top trending breed. In Canada, the adorable and feisty Yorkshire Terrier takes the top spot for trending breeds.\n",
      "\n",
      "In the United Kingdom, the Patterdale Terrier is rising in popularity and the active [Miniature American Shepherd](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/miniature-australian-shepherd/) is trending in France.\n",
      "\n",
      "In Spain, posh Toy Poodles are becoming more popular, while in Germany, the cute yet confident [Pugs](https://www.rover.com/blog/breeds/pug/) are rising in the ranks.\n",
      "\n",
      "## Top Trending Dog Breeds by US City\n",
      "\n",
      "![Yellow and green stars](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20296%20152'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%201024%20536'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "![A photo of a Bernedoodle dog on Rover](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20viewBox='0%200%20500%20452'%3E%3C/svg%3E)\n",
      "\n",
      "### Dolly the Bernedoodle\n",
      "\n",
      "“Dolly is our first dog together, and we feel like we couldn’t have asked for a better first pet. I grew up with Golden Retrievers and thought a Bernedoodle might have similar traits, such as loving to play fetch, but Dolly couldn’t be less bothered to play fetch! She loves to run around, spend time with her people and go on car rides. One thing to know about Bernedoodles is they require a lot of grooming and would probably do best in a cooler location during the summer months. Dolly loves to sleep belly up next to the fan in the summer, even here in Michigan!”\n",
      "\n",
      "— Brooke and Silas, Dog Parents on Rover, [Ann Arbor MI](https://www.rover.com/ann-arbor--mi--dog-boarding/)\n",
      "\n",
      "## Read More City Breed Trends\n",
      "\n",
