      "[Back To News](https://www.akc.org/expert-advice/news/)\n",
      "\n",
      "# The Most Popular Dog Breeds of 2023\n",
      "\n",
      "By [Melanie Haid](https://www.akc.org/author/mxhakc-org/)\n",
      "\n",
      "Updated: Apr 09, 2024 \\| 4 Minutes\n",
      "\n",
      "Updated: Apr 09, 2024 \\| 4 Minutes\n",
      "\n",
      "![French Bulldog puppy sitting on the floor in the living room.](https://www.akc.org/wp-content/themes/akc/component-library/assets/img/1x1.trans.gif)\n",
      "\n",
      "©Olesya - stock.adobe.com\n",
      "\n",
      "- [labrador retriever](https://www.akc.org/tag/labrador-retriever/)\n",
      "- [french bulldog](https://www.akc.org/tag/french-bulldog/)\n",
      "- [most popular breeds](https://www.akc.org/tag/most-popular-breeds/)\n",
      "\n",
      "As the world’s largest registry of purebred dogs, the AKC uses our registration statistics to rank the most popular breeds of the past year — and the numbers are in! Which breed has been named the American Kennel Club’s most popular dog breed of 2023?\n",
      "\n",
      "For the second year in a row, the [French Bulldog](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/french-bulldog/) has secured the No.1 spot as the most popular dog breed in the United States! Before 2022, the [Labrador Retriever](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/labrador-retriever/) had a firm hold on the top spot, keeping an impressive [31-year reign.](https://www.akc.org/expert-advice/dog-breeds/most-popular-dog-breeds-of-2021/)\n",
      "\n",
      "## Staying in the No.1 Spot\n",
      "\n",
      "The French Bulldog’s popularity shows no signs of slowing down. You’ve likely seen these bat-eared companions before, and they’re especially popular in cities. Over the last 11 years, the French Bulldog has steadily grown in registration rankings, ranking No. 14 in 2012. In 2021, Frenchies made it to the No. 2 spot, taking over the [top spot officially in 2022](https://www.akc.org/expert-advice/dog-breeds/most-popular-dog-breeds-2022/).\n",
      "\n",
      "![](https://www.akc.org/wp-content/themes/akc/component-library/assets/img/1x1.trans.gif)\n",
      "\n",
      "Surfman for WKC\n",
      "\n",
      "French Bulldog “Winston” won first place in the Non-Sporting Group, moving on to Best in Show at the 2022 AKC National Championship. Winston went on to win Best in Show.\n",
      "\n",
      "Part of the French Bulldog’s appeal is their playful, smart, and adaptable nature. They’re easy to bring anywhere, and great companions for a variety of lifestyles. These smart little dogs originated in France, and [French Bulldog history](https://www.akc.org/expert-advice/dog-breeds/breed-history-french-bulldog/) is one with many layers. Even in the past, they belonged to all kinds of people, from royalty to middle-class artisans. They were recognized by the American Kennel Club in 1898, giving them over 100 years of AKC history along with it.\n",
      "\n",
      "## Top 10 Dog Breeds of 2023\n",
      "\n",
      "It’s no surprise to dog lovers that the Frenchie remains in the No. 1 spot, and the rest of the top five breeds follow a similar pattern. Same as 2022, the Labrador Retriever, [Golden Retriever](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/golden-retriever/), [German Shepherd Dog](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/german-shepherd-dog/), and [Poodle](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/poodle-standard/) are ranked No. 2-5 respectively.\n",
      "\n",
      "There’s some shuffling in the top 10 from 2022 to 2023. The [Dachshund](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/dachshund/) is making moves, jumping from the No. 9 spot in 2022 to the No. 6 spot in 2023. [Rottweilers](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/rottweiler/) took their spot at No. 9 in 2023, moving down from No. 7 in 2022. [Bulldogs](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/bulldog/) went from No. 6 in 2022 to No. 7 in 2023. The [Beagle](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/beagle/) remains at No. 8, and the [German Shorthaired Pointer](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/german-shorthaired-pointer/) remains at No. 10 with no change in rank.\n",
      "\n",
      "![Beagle head portrait in a field in early morning.](https://www.akc.org/wp-content/themes/akc/component-library/assets/img/1x1.trans.gif)\n",
      "\n",
      "©Dogs - stock.adobe.com\n",
      "\n",
      "The top 10 breeds aren’t the only ones getting people’s attention. Other breeds working their way up the rank in 2023 were the [Papillion](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/papillon/), the [Great Pyrenees](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/great-pyrenees/), the [Basenji](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/basenji/), and the [Finnish Lapphund](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/finnish-lapphund/). From 2022 to 2023, the Finnish Lapphund went from No. 167 to No. 135, a total of 32 spots. The Basenji jumped 10 spots from No. 91 to No. 81, and the The Papillion went up six ranks from No. 51 t0 No. 45. The Great Pyrenees went up five spots from 2022 to 2023, going from No. 69 to No. 64.\n",
      "\n",
      "Over the last decade, breeds like the [Cane Corso](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/cane-corso/), [Belgian Malinois](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/belgian-malinois/), [Giant Schnauzer](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/giant-schnauzer/), and [English Cocker Spaniel](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/english-cocker-spaniel/) have also seen jumps in popularity. The [Mudi](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/mudi/), [Russian Toy](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/russian-toy/), and [Bracco Italiano](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/bracco-italiano/) were recognized in 2022, but since the Bracco Italiano was recognized in July of that year, they debuted in the registration lists in 2023, at No. 152. The Mudi went from its debut at No. 150 in 2022 to No. 130 in 2023, while the Russian Toy debuted at No. 144 and now ranks No. 141.\n",
      "\n",
      "The [Lancashire Heeler](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/lancashire-heeler/) was the [newest breed recognized by the AKC](https://www.akc.org/expert-advice/dog-breeds/lancashire-heeler-newest-akc-recognized-breed/) starting January 2024. Tune in next year to see where they debut in the ranks!\n",
      "\n",
      "## Least Popular Dog Breeds of 2023\n",
      "\n",
      "Ranked in the bottom five of the 200 breeds were the [Azawakh](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/azawakh/) (No. 196), the [Grand Basset Griffon Vendéen](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/grand-basset-griffon-vendeen/) (No. 197), [Norwegian Lundehund](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/norwegian-lundehund/) (No. 198), [English Foxhound](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/english-foxhound/) (No. 199), and [Sloughi](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/sloughi/) (No. 200). In 2021, the Norwegian Lundehund took the bottom spot, and has been making its way slowly up the rankings, passing the English Foxhound and Sloughi in 2023.\n",
      "\n",
      "The American Kennel Club has recognized 201 breeds since it was first founded in 1884, with additional breeds working their way towards recognition [AKC Foundation Stock Service](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/foundation-stock-service/) (FSS) and the [Miscellaneous Class](https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/miscellaneous-class/). If you’re looking for a puppy of your own, [AKC Marketplace](https://marketplace.akc.org/) can connect you with a responsible breeder.\n",
      "\n",
      "![slowmotiongli/Getty Images Plus](https://www.akc.org/wp-content/themes/akc/component-library/assets/img/1x1.trans.gif)\n",
      "\n",
      "slowmotiongli/Getty Images Plus\n",
      "\n",
      "The Norwegian Lundehund, recognized in 2011, was ranked No. 198 in the 2023 breed rankings.\n",
      "\n",
      "Knowing your dog will be counted as part of the compilation of the annual breed popularity list is only a small benefit of dog registration. The AKC is the only purebred dog registry in the United States that maintains systematic inspections and investigations. The AKC conducts thousands of inspections each year to ensure compliance with standards that support the welfare, safety, and health of dogs across the nation. The AKC and its affiliates have also [donated over $67 million to canine health research](https://www.akcchf.org/news-events/news/akc-canine-health-foundation-82.html) as of 2023, and the [Canine Health Foundation published their 1000th scientific publication](https://www.akcchf.org/news-events/news/akc-canine-health-foundation-79.html) in 2023.\n",
      "\n",
